{"id": "68a18c65-3498-486e-8553-210e9356140c", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T02:53:28.618081", "source": "user", "parent_id": "b32a4f42-5df1-4337-8e5f-a8c57a293c74", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Phase: initial_inspection. The submission contract calls for a final experiment design (rationale, controls, analysis plan, feasibility) covering how combined jailbreak techniques — varying attacker strength, defense strength, and prior-tested tricks — affect Attack Success Rate (ASR) under both token-level (AutoDAN) and prompt-level (PAIR) attacks, and how attacker-power × defense-robustness interactions differ across datasets and attack styles. Execution is NOT required; this is a design-only deliverable. Please do NOT produce the full design yet. For this turn I want only three things: (1) Repository and input inspection — list the contents of /workspace, read track.md verbatim, and summarize any documents, datasets, or code stubs already present. Confirm whether the runtime (bubblewrap, network disabled, CPU only, no preinstalled deps) supports any optional sanity-checks or if everything must be paper-only. (2) A proposed approach outline — propose (a) the independent variables and their levels (attacker strength, defense strength, prior-tricks/memory factor), (b) the fixed cells (AutoDAN for token-level, PAIR for prompt-level, datasets under consideration and rationale for choosing a small subset), (c) the response variable and how ASR will be operationalized (attack-budget-normalized? judge model dependent?), (d) candidate controls (prompt templates, seeds, judge model, refusal taxonomy), and (e) the intended statistical analysis matched to the factorial structure (main effects, two-way interactions, possible three-way if prior-tricks × defense × attacker is crossed). (3) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties — enumerate the design choices that materially change the conclusion (e.g., dataset selection, judge model choice, whether ‘prior-tricks’ is operationalized as cross-prompt memory within a session or as transfer from a held-out set, treatment of stochastic decoding, handling of borderline refusals, power analysis assumptions for detecting interactions, and feasibility ceiling given no GPU/network). Do not fabricate source-supported constraints from outside the visible documents; label anything you assume rather than know. Proceed.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"How do combined jailbreak techniques—involving attacker strength, defense strength, and previously tested tricks—affect the Attack Success Rate (ASR) of LLMs under both token-level (AutoDAN) and prompt-level (PAIR) attacks? What are the interaction dynamics between attacker power and defense robustness across datasets and attack styles?\", \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\", \"environment\": {\"runtime\": \"bubblewrap\", \"network\": \"disabled\", \"repository\": \"/workspace\", \"python\": \"python3\", \"software_environment\": \"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\", \"datasets\": [], \"gpu_enabled\": false, \"cpu_affinity_count\": 24, \"note\": \"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\"}, \"researcher_topics\": [\"neurips2024/97431/11:setup_17\", \"neurips2024/97431/11:setup_18\"], \"question_budget\": 6, \"actor_step_budget\": 60, \"continue_after_initial_plan\": false}}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 1}
