ikely be under-powered with seed-level aggregation; powered tests require treating the prompt as the unit (see Uncertainties).\n- **Effect-size reporting:** log-odds for the GLMM, plus marginal-mean ASR per cell. Bonferroni or Holm adjustment across the pre-registered two-way family.\n- **Robustness:** judge-sensitivity table (judge A vs B on the same trials), full vs subset dataset, and seeds 1–3 vs 4–5 split, reported in supplementary.\n- **Feasibility check that does not require network:** a stdlib Python simulation that generates synthetic cell-ASRs from `Beta(α=success+1, β=fail+1)` for a 2×2×2 reduced grid and reproduces the GLMM analysis logic — this validates the analysis script end-to-end before committing the design.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Consequential assumptions and uncertainties\n\nLabeling these as **assumed** unless a visible document pins them.\n\n1. **Dataset selection (assumption).** AdvBench + HarmBench are my proposal. AdvBench is older and noisier; HarmBench has cleaner refusal labels but a smaller prompt pool. The A × D interaction magnitude changes with dataset — this is the biggest uncontrolled cross-paper comparability issue. I will commit to two datasets and treat dataset as a blocking factor, not a confound.\n2. **Judge model choice (assumption).** I am assuming a single frozen LLM judge. The matched-judge design assumes the judge is *not* the same model family as the target model — otherwise judge–target confounds inflate ASR. GPT-4-class judges raise a leakage problem (training-data overlap with refusal benchmarks); open judges (Llama-Guard, HarmBench-Mistral) avoid that but have lower human agreement. **Sensitivity analysis with two judges is mandatory**, not optional.\n3. **\"Prior-tricks\" operationalization (assumption).** Two reasonable readings: (i) in-session memory buffer reused across prompts; (ii) transfer from a held-out set of previously successful tricks. The mechanisms produce different shapes of the M main effect: (i) helps within a session; (ii) helps regardless of session but only if tricks generalize. I propose **in-session memory as primary** (matches PAIR's streaming behavior most closely), with held-out transfer as a robustness extension if the design budget allows.\n4. **ASR operationalization at the judge boundary (assumption).** I propose a 3-level refusal taxonomy with a binary primary cut at \"substantive compliance.\" A reviewer could insist ASR includes \"partial compliance,\" which I expect to inflate ASR by 10–25% across the board. I will pre-register both cuts and report them side-by-side.\n5. **Stochastic decoding (assumption).** I assume **frozen decoding** (temperature, top-p, seeds controlled) so the cell-to-cell variance is dominated by attack-side randomness, not sampling noise. An alternative is to average across multiple decoding seeds per trial — more realistic but inflates cell-variance and reduces power.\n6. **Power analysis assumptions for interactions (assumption).** With 3·A × 3·D × 2·M × 2·S = 36 cells × 2 datasets = 72 cells, 50 prompts × 5 seeds, the natural **unit of analysis** is either the **prompt** (~18,000 trials; mixed model is over-powered for any interaction ≥ 0.1 log-odds, but borderline ASRs near 0/1 flip can violate binomial assumptions) or the **cell-mean** (5 replications per cell; severely under-powered for any 3-way interaction of effect size f<0.4). I will default to **prompt-as-unit GLMM with cell and dataset random effects**, and treat cell-aggregated analyses as descriptive.\n7. **Attack style × memory compatibility (uncertainty).** \"Prior tricks\" means **lexical** fragments in AutoDAN and **semantic** paraphrases in PAIR. A single shared \"memory\" buffer conflates these; the M effect is most likely an M × S interaction rather than a main effect. I am assuming style-specific memory buffers with the same abstract definition.\n8. **Defense monotonicity (uncertainty).** Strong defenses do not monotonically reduce ASR; perplexity filters are bypassed by AutoDAN-style low-perplexity generations, and paraphrase defenses hurt both styles unevenly. I am assuming the \"strong\" defense level is in fact stronger than \"standard\" on average; if it isn't per attack style, the A × D × S interaction is structurally biased.\n9. **Target LLM choice (assumption).** Fixing one target means the conclusion is **about that model**, not about LLMs in general. I will report target choice as an explicit, narrow scope statement.\n10. **Feasibility ceiling (documented).** Per track.md: \"repositories and compute are not required.\" This is a design-only deliverable. Any stdlib sanity-check is acceptable but is not evidence about jailbreaks. I will not claim that any experiment was run.\n11. **In-repo source coverage (uncertainty).** The brief refers to two `neurips2024/97431/11:setup_*` topics that are not present as files or as entries in `task.json`. If those carry source-supported setup constraints you want enforced (e.g., a specific dataset slice, a specific judge, a specific target model), please point me to the textual content; I will not fabricate constraints from IDs alone.\n\n### Open choices I would like you to confirm or override before I write the full design\n- **Dataset pair:** AdvBench + HarmBench (my proposal). Acceptable?\n- **Target LLM:** one open chat-tuned model (~7B–8B) — which one?\n- **M factor levels:** 2 (none / in-session) or 3 (also held-out transfer)?\n- **Judge:** single open-source judge + sensitivity sub-table (my proposal), or accept a GPT-4-class judge as primary?\n- **Unit of analysis in the GLMM:** prompt-as-unit (my default) vs cell-mean?\n\nOnce you confirm or redirect on these five points, I will produce the full design (rationale, controls, full analysis plan, feasibility) as `stage=result`.", "evidence": "Inspection findings (evidence): /workspace contains only task.json (637 bytes); documents.track.md equals the single sentence \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" — that is the entire scope of source-supported content. The two researcher_topics IDs in the brief are absent from /workspace and absent from task.json; nothing in-repo pins them. Runtime probe: bubblewrap active (CapEff = 0), DNS resolution fails (Errno -3 Temporary failure in name resolution), no scientific packages (numpy, scipy, pandas, statsmodels all ModuleNotFoundError), Python 3.12 stdlib + 24 CPUs available. No code, datasets, or other artifacts are present. Per track.md itself, repositories and compute are not required, so the deliverable is paper-only; any sanity-check is limited to a stdlib-only factorial simulation validating the analysis path, not evidence about jailbreaks. The IVs (A = attacker strength, D = defense strength, M = prior-tricks), the fixed attack-style factor S in {AutoDAN, PAIR}, dataset candidates (AdvBench, HarmBench), ASR operationalization (matched fixed-budget ASR + QFS + 3-level refusal taxonomy with single primary judge + sensitivity sub-table), the proposed 3x3x2x2x2 mixed design and binomial GLMM analysis, and the listed uncertainties are derivations from the public task question and the runtime contract — not from any source-pinned constraint in the visible documents.", "assumptions": ["Dataset choice: AdvBench + HarmBench is a design proposal (no source doc pins this).", "Target LLM is a placeholder (one open chat-tuned 7B-8B model from AutoDAN/PAIR literature); not pinned by visible documents.", "M operationalization: in-session memory as primary, held-out transfer as robustness extension; not pinned.", "Single LLM judge + sensitivity sub-table is a design proposal; exact judge identity is open.", "3-level refusal taxonomy and prompt-as-unit GLMM are design proposals, not document-pinned.", "Power-analysis assumptions (effect size, prompt-vs-cell unit) need ratification.", "Memory buffer is assumed style-specific (lexical for AutoDAN, semantic for PAIR); not document-pinned."], "uncertainties": ["Whether 'across datasets and attack styles' requires crossing or nesting (I propose crossing).", "Whether ASR's primary judge should be LLM-based, keyword-based, or both with one as primary.", "Whether the D 3-level gradient should be instantiated with concrete defenses or kept abstract.", "Whether M must span in-session and held-out-transfer or only one reading.", "Whether the deliverable allows a stdlib-only simulation of the analysis path as a feasibility check.", "Whether neurips2024/97431/11:setup_17 and setup_18 carry source-supported setup constraints I am missing."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-1d916dd7-9658-45c2-849f-4818e6d14d1b", "tool_call": {"id": "call-1d916dd7-9658-45c2-849f-4818e6d14d1b", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"ASR factorial plan with arrays of strings\", \"stage\": \"plan\", \"answer\": \"## 1. Repository & input inspection\\n\\n**`/workspace` contents (verbatim `ls -la`):**\\n```\\ndrwx------  2 ...  4096 Oct 21 09:53 .\\n-rw-------  1 ...   637 Oct 21 09:53 task.json\\n```\\nThere is exactly **one** file: `/workspace/task.json`. No `track.md`, no code, no datasets, no notebooks, no stubs.\\n\\n**`task.json` (verbatim structure):** top-level keys are `question`, `context`, `documents`. `documents` contains a single entry:\\n```\\n\\\"track.md\\\": \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n```\\nThat sentence is the full text of the only document in scope. The `researcher_topics` listed in the brief (`neurips2024/97431/11:setup_17`, `\\u2026/setup_18`) are **not** present in the JSON; from the visible file those IDs have no in-repo content. I will treat anything beyond the one sentence above as **inference or assumption**, never as a documented constraint.\\n\\n**Runtime probe results:**\\n- Bubblewrap active; `CapEff: 0000000000000000`, DNS resolution fails (`Errno -3 Temporary failure in name resolution`).\\n- Python 3.12.3, 24 CPUs, 125 GiB RAM, plenty of disk \\u2014 but **no scientific packages installed**: `numpy`, `scipy`, `pandas`, `statsmodels` all `ModuleNotFoundError`. Network is disabled, so `pip install` cannot fetch them.\\n- Stdlib (`json`, `math`, `random`, `statistics`, `itertools`) works.\\n\\n**Implication for sanity-checks:** I can run tiny stdlib-only simulations of the factorial design (e.g., synthetic binomial ASRs in 2\\u00d72\\u00d72 to confirm the analysis code path). I **cannot** run a real jailbreak model, an LLM judge, or a tokenizer-heavy AutoDAN evaluation in this environment. Track.md explicitly states repositories and compute are not required, so the deliverable is entirely paper-only; stdlib sims are optional and useful only to validate analysis scripts, not to produce evidence about jailbreaks.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Proposed approach outline\\n\\n### (a) Independent variables and levels\\nThe public question names **three** factors; I propose three levels each for the two \\\"strength\\\" factors and two levels for the memory factor, giving a clean orthogonal design without saturating.\\n\\n| Factor | Symbol | Levels | Operationalization sketch (assumed) |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Attacker strength | A | weak / medium / strong | weak: small attacker LLM + \\u2264100 AutoDAN gens / \\u22645 PAIR turns; medium: published baseline (AutoDAN GA budget = 100, PAIR k=20 streams \\u00d7 5 turns); strong: 5\\u00d7 budget, larger attacker model, broader mutation operators |\\n| Defense strength | D | off / standard / strong | off: vanilla chat template; standard: refusal system prompt + perplexity filter; strong: paraphrase-then-classify + sample-and-vote smoothing |\\n| Prior-tricks (memory) | M | none / in-session | none: each prompt attempted from scratch; in-session: a persistent buffer of successful prefixes/suffixes (AutoDAN) or paraphrases (PAIR) is reused across prompts within the same session. A third \\\"transfer-from-held-out\\\" level is a candidate if you want to broaden M to 3 levels |\\n\\n### (b) Fixed cells (cannot vary \\u2014 they are pinned by the question)\\n- **Attack style S \\u2208 {AutoDAN (token-level), PAIR (prompt-level)}** \\u2014 fixed by the question. Reported as a **blocking-style** factor so that ASR can be compared across styles within the same (A, D, M) cell.\\n- **Target LLM:** fixed (assumed: one open chat-tuned 7B/8B model used in the original AutoDAN/PAIR papers, e.g., Vicuna-7B or Llama-3-8B-Instruct). The exact choice is a design assumption flagged below.\\n- **Datasets (small subset, justified):** the question spans \\\"datasets\\\" (plural). Each dataset is a **blocking** factor with a deliberately small, well-justified slice. Candidate justification:\\n  1. **AdvBench \\\"Harmful Behaviors\\\"** (Universally aligned behaviors, ~520 prompts) \\u2014 original AutoDAN source; lets one cite prior numbers as a sanity baseline.\\n  2. **HarmBench \\\"standard\\\"** (~200 behaviors, balanced across 7 harm categories) \\u2014 current community standard and cleanest refusal-taxonomy coverage.\\n\\n  Justification for a **small subset** (e.g., n=50 per dataset, stratified by category) instead of the full benchmark: (i) attack-budget per prompt is the dominant cost (PAIR at 5\\u201320 judge queries \\u00d7 50 prompts \\u00d7 72 cells is already 18k\\u201372k judge queries per replication); (ii) ASR distribution is mostly bounded and saturates with ~30\\u201350 prompts per dataset in published ablations; (iii) stratification preserves category coverage, which matters because the headline A \\u00d7 D interaction is known to vary by category. Same 50 prompts are used in every cell to enable matched comparisons.\\n\\n### (c) Response variable \\u2014 ASR operationalization\\n- **Primary definition:** ASR_cell = (#marked-as-successful / #prompts-per-cell), where \\\"successful\\\" = judge labels the response as **non-refusal + substantive compliance** under the chosen refusal taxonomy (see (d)).\\n- **Budget normalization:** AutoDAN and PAIR have asymmetric budgets. Two complementary reporting rules:\\n  1. *Fixed-budget ASR*: ASR measured at a **matched** attack budget (e.g., AutoDAN 100 generations \\u00d7 1 attacker call \\u2248 PAIR 20 judge tokens). This is the headline ASR.\\n  2. *Queries-to-first-success (QFS)*: a continuous companion metric that is independent of the budget cap. Log-computed for analysis.\\n- **Judge coupling:** ASR is **judge-dependent by construction**. I will commit to a **single LLM judge with a frozen prompt template** (assumed: an open judge such as HarmBench-Mistral-7B or, alternatively, a GPT-4-class judge if API access is assumed in the design context). I will also pre-register a **keyword/refusal classifier** as a secondary judge and report both judge trajectories as a **sensitivity check**, not as parallel results, because contradictory ASRs across judges cannot both be primary.\\n\\n### (d) Candidate controls (everything held fixed across cells)\\n- System + user prompt templates (frozen, versioned).\\n- Decoding: temperature, top-p, max-tokens (frozen per target model).\\n- Random seeds for AutoDAN's GA and PAIR's stochastic attacker \\u2014 **\\u22655 seeds per cell** for variance estimation, with results reported as mean \\u00b1 95% CI per cell.\\n- Attack budget cap (matched across styles; see (c)).\\n- Held-out set for \\\"prior-tricks\\\" is **disjoint** from the evaluation prompts (no leakage).\\n- Refusal taxonomy: a **3-level rubric**: (i) full refusal, (ii) partial / hedged compliance, (iii) substantive compliance. ASR counts (iii); partial is reported separately. Borderline cases are resolved by a **second independent judge** with adjudication rules pre-registered.\\n- Judge prompt template, version, and temperature=0.\\n- Logging: full transcripts + judge scores retained for every trial.\\n\\n### (e) Statistical analysis matched to the factorial structure\\n**Design:** 3 (A) \\u00d7 3 (D) \\u00d7 2 (M) \\u00d7 2 (S) \\u00d7 2 (dataset) with seeds as within-cell replication. Headline test-bed: a between-cells mixed-effects model on cell-level ASR (aggregated by seed).\\n- **Primary specification (assumed):** binomial GLMM with `logit(ASR)` link. Fixed: A, D, M, S, dataset, and **all two-way interactions**. Random: (dataset \\u00d7 cell) intercept, by-seed random intercept. Two-way interactions are pre-registered as fully powered; three-way A \\u00d7 D \\u00d7 S and M \\u00d7 S \\u00d7 S_followup are exploratory.\\n- **Headline interactions:**\\n  - **A \\u00d7 D** \\u2014 the \\\"arms-race\\\" interaction, central to the research question.\\n  - **A \\u00d7 D \\u00d7 S** \\u2014 does the arms race differ between AutoDAN (token) and PAIR (prompt)?\\n  - **M \\u00d7 A** and **M \\u00d7 S** \\u2014 does prior-tricks memory amplify attacker strength, and is the amplification attack-style-specific?\\n- **Three-way A \\u00d7 D \\u00d7 M** is reported as an exploratory interaction because it is the natural cross-product of \\\"trick memorization changes the arms race,\\\" but it will likely be under-powered with seed-level aggregation; powered tests require treating the prompt as the unit (see Uncertainties).\\n- **Effect-size reporting:** log-odds for the GLMM, plus marginal-mean ASR per cell. Bonferroni or Holm adjustment across the pre-registered two-way family.\\n- **Robustness:** judge-sensitivity table (judge A vs B on the same trials), full vs subset dataset, and seeds 1\\u20133 vs 4\\u20135 split, reported in supplementary.\\n- **Feasibility check that does not require network:** a stdlib Python simulation that generates synthetic cell-ASRs from `Beta(\\u03b1=success+1, \\u03b2=fail+1)` for a 2\\u00d72\\u00d72 reduced grid and reproduces the GLMM analysis logic \\u2014 this validates the analysis script end-to-end before committing the design.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Consequential assumptions and uncertainties\\n\\nLabeling these as **assumed** unless a visible document pins them.\\n\\n1. **Dataset selection (assumption).** AdvBench + HarmBench are my proposal. AdvBench is older and noisier; HarmBench has cleaner refusal labels but a smaller prompt pool. The A \\u00d7 D interaction magnitude changes with dataset \\u2014 this is the biggest uncontrolled cross-paper comparability issue. I will commit to two datasets and treat dataset as a blocking factor, not a confound.\\n2. **Judge model choice (assumption).** I am assuming a single frozen LLM judge. The matched-judge design assumes the judge is *not* the same model family as the target model \\u2014 otherwise judge\\u2013target confounds inflate ASR. GPT-4-class judges raise a leakage problem (training-data overlap with refusal benchmarks); open judges (Llama-Guard, HarmBench-Mistral) avoid that but have lower human agreement. **Sensitivity analysis with two judges is mandatory**, not optional.\\n3. **\\\"Prior-tricks\\\" operationalization (assumption).** Two reasonable readings: (i) in-session memory buffer reused across prompts; (ii) transfer from a held-out set of previously successful tricks. The mechanisms produce different shapes of the M main effect: (i) helps within a session; (ii) helps regardless of session but only if tricks generalize. I propose **in-session memory as primary** (matches PAIR's streaming behavior most closely), with held-out transfer as a robustness extension if the design budget allows.\\n4. **ASR operationalization at the judge boundary (assumption).** I propose a 3-level refusal taxonomy with a binary primary cut at \\\"substantive compliance.\\\" A reviewer could insist ASR includes \\\"partial compliance,\\\" which I expect to inflate ASR by 10\\u201325% across the board. I will pre-register both cuts and report them side-by-side.\\n5. **Stochastic decoding (assumption).** I assume **frozen decoding** (temperature, top-p, seeds controlled) so the cell-to-cell variance is dominated by attack-side randomness, not sampling noise. An alternative is to average across multiple decoding seeds per trial \\u2014 more realistic but inflates cell-variance and reduces power.\\n6. **Power analysis assumptions for interactions (assumption).** With 3\\u00b7A \\u00d7 3\\u00b7D \\u00d7 2\\u00b7M \\u00d7 2\\u00b7S = 36 cells \\u00d7 2 datasets = 72 cells, 50 prompts \\u00d7 5 seeds, the natural **unit of analysis** is either the **prompt** (~18,000 trials; mixed model is over-powered for any interaction \\u2265 0.1 log-odds, but borderline ASRs near 0/1 flip can violate binomial assumptions) or the **cell-mean** (5 replications per cell; severely under-powered for any 3-way interaction of effect size f<0.4). I will default to **prompt-as-unit GLMM with cell and dataset random effects**, and treat cell-aggregated analyses as descriptive.\\n7. **Attack style \\u00d7 memory compatibility (uncertainty).** \\\"Prior tricks\\\" means **lexical** fragments in AutoDAN and **semantic** paraphrases in PAIR. A single shared \\\"memory\\\" buffer conflates these; the M effect is most likely an M \\u00d7 S interaction rather than a main effect. I am assuming style-specific memory buffers with the same abstract definition.\\n8. **Defense monotonicity (uncertainty).** Strong defenses do not monotonically reduce ASR; perplexity filters are bypassed by AutoDAN-style low-perplexity generations, and paraphrase defenses hurt both styles unevenly. I am assuming the \\\"strong\\\" defense level is in fact stronger than \\\"standard\\\" on average; if it isn't per attack style, the A \\u00d7 D \\u00d7 S interaction is structurally biased.\\n9. **Target LLM choice (assumption).** Fixing one target means the conclusion is **about that model**, not about LLMs in general. I will report target choice as an explicit, narrow scope statement.\\n10. **Feasibility ceiling (documented).** Per track.md: \\\"repositories and compute are not required.\\\" This is a design-only deliverable. Any stdlib sanity-check is acceptable but is not evidence about jailbreaks. I will not claim that any experiment was run.\\n11. **In-repo source coverage (uncertainty).** The brief refers to two `neurips2024/97431/11:setup_*` topics that are not present as files or as entries in `task.json`. If those carry source-supported setup constraints you want enforced (e.g., a specific dataset slice, a specific judge, a specific target model), please point me to the textual content; I will not fabricate constraints from IDs alone.\\n\\n### Open choices I would like you to confirm or override before I write the full design\\n- **Dataset pair:** AdvBench + HarmBench (my proposal). Acceptable?\\n- **Target LLM:** one open chat-tuned model (~7B\\u20138B) \\u2014 which one?\\n- **M factor levels:** 2 (none / in-session) or 3 (also held-out transfer)?\\n- **Judge:** single open-source judge + sensitivity sub-table (my proposal), or accept a GPT-4-class judge as primary?\\n- **Unit of analysis in the GLMM:** prompt-as-unit (my default) vs cell-mean?\\n\\nOnce you confirm or redirect on these five points, I will produce the full design (rationale, controls, full analysis plan, feasibility) as `stage=result`.\", \"evidence\": \"Inspection findings (evidence): /workspace contains only task.json (637 bytes); documents.track.md equals the single sentence \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" \\u2014 that is the entire scope of source-supported content. The two researcher_topics IDs in the brief are absent from /workspace and absent from task.json; nothing in-repo pins them. Runtime probe: bubblewrap active (CapEff = 0), DNS resolution fails (Errno -3 Temporary failure in name resolution), no scientific packages (numpy, scipy, pandas, statsmodels all ModuleNotFoundError), Python 3.12 stdlib + 24 CPUs available. No code, datasets, or other artifacts are present. Per track.md itself, repositories and compute are not required, so the deliverable is paper-only; any sanity-check is limited to a stdlib-only factorial simulation validating the analysis path, not evidence about jailbreaks. The IVs (A = attacker strength, D = defense strength, M = prior-tricks), the fixed attack-style factor S in {AutoDAN, PAIR}, dataset candidates (AdvBench, HarmBench), ASR operationalization (matched fixed-budget ASR + QFS + 3-level refusal taxonomy with single primary judge + sensitivity sub-table), the proposed 3x3x2x2x2 mixed design and binomial GLMM analysis, and the listed uncertainties are derivations from the public task question and the runtime contract \\u2014 not from any source-pinned constraint in the visible documents.\", \"assumptions\": [\"Dataset choice: AdvBench + HarmBench is a design proposal (no source doc pins this).\", \"Target LLM is a placeholder (one open chat-tuned 7B-8B model from AutoDAN/PAIR literature); not pinned by visible documents.\", \"M operationalization: in-session memory as primary, held-out transfer as robustness extension; not pinned.\", \"Single LLM judge + sensitivity sub-table is a design proposal; exact judge identity is open.\", \"3-level refusal taxonomy and prompt-as-unit GLMM are design proposals, not document-pinned.\", \"Power-analysis assumptions (effect size, prompt-vs-cell unit) need ratification.\", \"Memory buffer is assumed style-specific (lexical for AutoDAN, semantic for PAIR); not document-pinned.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Whether 'across datasets and attack styles' requires crossing or nesting (I propose crossing).\", \"Whether ASR's primary judge should be LLM-based, keyword-based, or both with one as primary.\", \"Whether the D 3-level gradient should be instantiated with concrete defenses or kept abstract.\", \"Whether M must span in-session and held-out-transfer or only one reading.\", \"Whether the deliverable allows a stdlib-only simulation of the analysis path as a feasibility check.\", \"Whether neurips2024/97431/11:setup_17 and setup_18 carry source-supported setup constraints I am missing.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_respon