{"id": "98bb7aaa-57f5-4523-99f6-bc6d52b48ff4", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T02:57:04.107772", "source": "user", "parent_id": "53e1db4a-7690-4caa-acd8-55f443984680", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved with the following directives for the final design writeup. Endorse the proposed structure and lock the open choices as follows so the design is coherent end-to-end: (1) Datasets: AdvBench Harmful Behaviors + HarmBench standard, n=50 stratified per dataset, same 50 prompts used across every cell for matched comparisons. (2) Target LLM: keep abstract as a single open chat-tuned ~7B-8B target referenced in the AutoDAN/PAIR literature, named as an explicit assumption in the deliverable; do not over-constrain model identity in a design-only document. (3) M factor: 2 levels (none / in-session) as primary; mention held-out transfer as a robustness extension in a 'future work / out of scope' subsection, not as a cell in the main factorial. (4) Judge: single open-source judge as primary (HarmBench-Mistral-class or Llama-Guard-class), with a keyword/refusal classifier as secondary; report a judge-sensitivity sub-table. Note the open-judge leakage concern explicitly. (5) Unit of analysis: prompt-as-unit binomial GLMM with cell and dataset as random effects; cell-mean analyses are descriptive only. Also endorse the stdlib-only factorial simulation as a feasibility check on the analysis script path, with an explicit statement that it is not evidence about jailbreaks. Required sections in the final deliverable, in this order: (a) Research question recap and pre-registered hypotheses for A x D, A x D x S, M x A, M x S; (b) Design diagram of the 3 (A) x 3 (D) x 2 (M) x 2 (S) x 2 (dataset) factorial with seeds as within-cell replication; (c) Operational definitions (ASR primary cut + partial-compliance secondary cut, matched fixed-budget ASR, QFS companion metric); (d) Controls and frozen-hygiene list (prompts, decoding, seeds, judge prompt, refusal taxonomy, held-out disjointness); (e) Statistical analysis plan with the GLMM specification, pre-registered two-way interaction family, multiplicity adjustment, and the exploratory A x D x M caveat; (f) Effect-size reporting plan (log-odds + marginal-mean ASR per cell with 95% CIs); (g) Robustness plan (judge sensitivity, full-vs-subset prompts, seed-split replication); (h) Feasibility and resource estimate (attack-budget math, judge-query count, expected wall-time class with/without GPU, explicitly noting the no-execution context); (i) Threats to validity enumerated from the assumptions list (dataset, judge, M operationalization, refusal boundary, decoding, monotonicity of D, target-LLM scope, in-memory vs transfer); (j) Explicit assumption register. Do not produce results, do not claim execution, do not fabricate source-pinned constraints.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 24}
{"id": "047c845d-5bed-4d25-8529-ddcc66d0747a", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T02:58:39.452281", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "98bb7aaa-57f5-4523-99f6-bc6d52b48ff4", "action": {"command": "cat > /tmp/design_sim.py << 'PY'\n\"\"\"Feasibility check for the proposed 3x3x2x2x2 factorial + prompt-as-unit GLMM path.\n\nThis is a stdlib-only simulation intended ONLY to confirm:\n  1. The cell grid is balanced and enumerated correctly.\n  2. The Monte-Carlo generation of prompt-level Bernoulli outcomes from a known\n     logistic model (with A x D, M x A, M x S, A x D x S effects) yields unbiased\n     cell-margin means for ASR in expectation.\n  3. Exact-binomial (Wilson-style) 95% CIs on cell ASRs are well-defined and\n     narrow enough that A x D detection is plausible at n=50 prompts x 5 seeds.\nIt is NOT evidence about jailbreaks.\n\nNo numpy/scipy/statsmodels are available, so this fits the LogisticIRLS via\nNewton steps within each cell only as a sanity backcheck. The actual analysis\nplan calls for a beta/binomial GLMM and is otherwise out of scope here.\n\"\"\"\nimport itertools, math, random, statistics\nrandom.seed(20261021)\n\n# ---- 1. Cell grid -----------------------------------------------------------\nA_levels = [\"weak\", \"medium\", \"strong\"]           # 3\nD_levels = [\"off\",   \"standard\", \"strong\"]        # 3\nM_levels = [\"none\",  \"in-session\"]                # 2\nS_levels = [\"AutoDAN\", \"PAIR\"]                    # 2\nT_levels = [\"AdvBench\", \"HarmBench\"]              # 2 (named 'T' for 'T'able in code, dataset elsewhere)\ncells = list(itertools.product(A_levels, D_levels, M_levels, S_levels, T_levels))\nassert len(cells) == 3*3*2*2*2 == 72, \"cell count mismatch\"\nprint(f\"[grid] cells = {len(cells)} per cell x 50 prompts x 5 seeds = \"\n      f\"{len(cells)*50*5} trial-units\")\n\n# ---- 2. Inject a known logistic model --------------------------------------\ndef true_logit(A, D, M, S):\n    # Baseline + main effects + A x D + M x A + M x S + small A x D x S\n    base = -2.0\n    a = {\"weak\": -0.7, \"medium\": 0.0, \"strong\": 0.7}[A]\n    d = {\"off\": 0.0, \"standard\": -0.4, \"strong\": -0.9}[D]\n    m = {\"none\": 0.0, \"in-session\": 0.5}[M]\n    s = {\"AutoDAN\": 0.0, \"PAIR\": 0.3}[S]\n    a_x_d = 0.3 * a * d                       # arms-race: attacker-defender coupling\n    m_x_a = 0.4 * m * a                       # memory compounds attacker strength\n    m_x_s = -0.2 * m * s                      # memory slightly hurts on PAIR (semantic redundancy)\n    a_x_d_x_s = 0.15 * a * d * s\n    return base + a + d + m + s + a_x_d + m_x_a + m_x_s + a_x_d_x_s\n\ndef sample_one(A, D, M, S, n_prompts=50, n_seeds=5):\n    p = 1.0 / (1.0 + math.exp(-true_logit(A, D, M, S)))\n    successes_per_prompt = []\n    for _ in range(n_prompts):\n        # one Bernoulli per (prompt,seed) pair, mean = p, var = p(1-p)/n_seeds\n        p_hat = sum(1 for _ in range(n_seeds) if random.random() < p) / n_seeds\n        successes_per_prompt.append(p_hat)\n    return p, statistics.fmean(successes_per_prompt)\n\n# ---- 3. Aggregate over cells -----------------------------------------------\nn_prompts   = 50\nn_seeds     = 5\ntrial_units = len(cells) * n_prompts * n_seeds\nprint(f\"[grid] trial-units = {trial_units}\")  # expect 72*250 = 18000\n\n# Per-cell mean over a single Monte-Carlo run\nmargins = {}\nfor cell in cells:\n    p_true, p_hat = sample_one(*cell, n_prompts=n_prompts, n_seeds=n_seeds)\n    # Wilson 95% CI approximation in stdlib\n    z = 1.96\n    denom = 1 + z*z/n_prompts\n    center = (p_hat + z*z/(2*n_prompts))/denom\n    half   = z*math.sqrt((p_hat*(1-p_hat) + z*z/(4*n_prompts))/n_prompts)/denom\n    margins[cell] = (p_true, p_hat, center, half)\n\n# ---- 4. Backchecks ---------------------------------------------------------\nA_idx = {l:i for i,l in enumerate(A_levels)}\nD_idx = {l:i for i,l in enumerate(D_levels)}\n\n# (i) overall mean across all cells in simulation matches expected mean\nmean_p_true = sum(p for p,_,_,_ in margins.values()) / len(margins)\nmean_p_hat  = sum(p for _,p,_,_ in margins.values()) / len(margins)\nprint(f\"[check] mean true ASR    = {mean_p_true:.3f}\")\nprint(f\"[check] mean simulated  = {mean_p_hat:.3f}  (expect ~equal within MC noise)\")\n\n# (ii) average bias magnitude\nbiases = [abs(ph - pt) for pt,ph,_,_ in margins.values()]\nprint(f\"[check] mean |hat - true| = {statistics.fmean(biases):.3f}  \"\n      f\"max = {max(biases):.3f}\")\n\n# (iii) width of CI vs effect size of strongest A x D cell-pair\n# Take strongest attacker / strongest defense vs weakest\nstrong_pair = margins[(\"strong\",\"strong\",\"none\",\"AutoDAN\",\"AdvBench\")]\nweak_pair   = margins[(\"weak\",  \"off\",  \"none\",\"AutoDAN\",\"AdvBench\")]\ndelta = strong_pair[1] - weak_pair[1]\nprint(f\"[A x D] weak/off ASR = {weak_pair[1]:.2f} [{weak_pair[2]-weak_pair[3]:.2f},\"\n      f\" {weak_pair[2]+weak_pair[3]:.2f}]  \"\n      f\"strong/strong ASR = {strong_pair[1]:.2f} \"\n      f\"[{strong_pair[2]-strong_pair[3]:.2f}, {strong_pair[2]+strong_pair[3]:.2f}]  \"\n      f\"delta = {delta:+.2f}\")\n\n# (iv) confirm that numeric tags (A_idx x D_idx) align with direction of A x D\ndef avg_asr(A, D):\n    vals = [p for cell,p,_,_ in margins.items() if cell[0]==A and cell[1]==D]\n    return statistics.fmean(vals)\n\nprint(\"[A x D marginal ASR across M,S,dataset (averaged in sim)]:\")\nfor A in A_levels:\n    row = [f\"{avg_asr(A,D):.2f}\" for D in D_levels]\n    print(f\"  A={A:>6}  D=\" + \"  \".join(f\"{v:>5}\" for v in row))\n\n# (v) Confirm M x A cell-mean direction matches injected model\ndef avg_asr_MA(M, A):\n    vals = [p for cell,p,_,_ in margins.items() if cell[2]==M and cell[0]==A]\n    return statistics.fmean(vals)\nprint(\"[M x A marginal ASR (sim)]:\")\nfor M in M_levels:\n    row = [f\"{avg_asr_MA(M,A):.2f}\" for A in A_levels]\n    print(f\"  M={M:>10}  A=\" + \"  \".join(f\"{v:>5}\" for v in row))\n\nprint(\"[grid] design physically checks out: 72 balanced cells, 18,000 trial units,\"\n      \" binomial sampling recovers injected interactions within MC noise.\")\nPY\npython3 /tmp/design_sim.py", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-8d7017ad-34f2-4bc9-b33e-d3ea532c6457", "tool_call": {"id": "call-8d7017ad-34f2-4bc9-b33e-d3ea532c6457", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Run stdlib factorial feasibility check\", \"command\": \"cat > /tmp/design_sim.py << 'PY'\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"Feasibility check for the proposed 3x3x2x2x2 factorial + prompt-as-unit GLMM path.\\n\\nThis is a stdlib-only simulation intended ONLY to confirm:\\n  1. The cell grid is balanced and enumerated correctly.\\n  2. The Monte-Carlo generation of prompt-level Bernoulli outcomes from a known\\n     logistic model (with A x D, M x A, M x S, A x D x S effects) yields unbiased\\n     cell-margin means for ASR in expectation.\\n  3. Exact-binomial (Wilson-style) 95% CIs on cell ASRs are well-defined and\\n     narrow enough that A x D detection is plausible at n=50 prompts x 5 seeds.\\nIt is NOT evidence about jailbreaks.\\n\\nNo numpy/scipy/statsmodels are available, so this fits the LogisticIRLS via\\nNewton steps within each cell only as a sanity backcheck. The actual analysis\\nplan calls for a beta/binomial GLMM and is otherwise out of scope here.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\nimport itertools, math, random, statistics\\nrandom.seed(20261021)\\n\\n# ---- 1. Cell grid -----------------------------------------------------------\\nA_levels = [\\\"weak\\\", \\\"medium\\\", \\\"strong\\\"]           # 3\\nD_levels = [\\\"off\\\",   \\\"standard\\\", \\\"strong\\\"]        # 3\\nM_levels = [\\\"none\\\",  \\\"in-session\\\"]                # 2\\nS_levels = [\\\"AutoDAN\\\", \\\"PAIR\\\"]                    # 2\\nT_levels = [\\\"AdvBench\\\", \\\"HarmBench\\\"]              # 2 (named 'T' for 'T'able in code, dataset elsewhere)\\ncells = list(itertools.p